    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, Srivijaya was a sea power that ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a> from the 7th to the 13th century.</li><li>It grew rich by taxing China-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> shipping and became a renowned centre of Buddhist learning, spreading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-malay">Old Malay</a> across the region.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-invasion-of-srivijaya">Chola raids</a>, Javanese rivals, direct Chinese shipping and the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> wore it down until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temasek">Temasek</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> took its role.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yijing">Yijing</a>, who stopped there in 671, found over a thousand Buddhist monks and advised Chinese monks to study in Srivijaya before going to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

